cies  and that could have caused all that  product to be implicated in the recall  which the customer did not want so this  was a perfect solution for them to be  able to figure out this challenge  without getting themselves into any kind  of regulatory Problems by doing the  analysis  so our last case study is with facility  B which is an ice cream manufacturer  that does routine monitoring for  Listeria species in their processing  environment  they also perform confirmation up to the  point of a listeria-like organism on a  plate  and so they wanted to actually begin  subtyping these listeria-like organisms  to be able to determine if they have one  strain or if they have multiple strains  and they also wanted to be able to  observe their contamination patterns  however they could not know which  listeria species these isolates were in  order to again avoid a recall situation  because the product that they had  produced was done while they had been  collecting these isolates and they  didn't want to implicate a new product  so we actually had uh isolates for  modified Oxford auger or MOX auger  plates  which were a typical listeria-like  organisms but they had not been  biochemically identified to the species  level they sent these isolates on plates  and these were frozen for banking at our  facility  at these various intervals isolates were  streaked to from these Frozen cultures  to tryptic soyager plates in order to  create wands and then we went through  the normal uh process of getting those  prepared for the broker Ira biotyper as  we mentioned in the previous case  studies  so over time listeria-like organisms  from this facility were grouped into  five different clusters these  represented five different strains that  were present in the facility  the largest cluster of these was cluster  184 that had 19 isolates in it followed  by cluster 182 with eight isolates uh  and the presence of these two clusters  demonstrated that there were two major  resonant strengths that had taken up  Harbor Bridge in the facility over time  cluster 164 cluster 146 uh each had two  isolates cluster 167 only had one  isolate so these were all presumed to be  some uh probably transient strains uh  more or less definitely not as  persistent as those two strains that  were represented by cluster 184 and  cluster 182.  so with this information a customer was  then able to plot all this subtyping  information on top of their positive  results that they were seeing with their  routine monitoring and were able to find  some of the issues that they were having  in contamination patterns that they were  seeing over time  so facility D actually took their  positive Point data from their  environmental monitoring software I wish  they'd get through us which I mentioned  is the the Emma program  they actually printed this off and then  added the subtyping information on top  of their positive spots on their Maps  this then allowed them to be able to  better visualize the distribution of  each strain throughout the facility  and so over time they kept doing this  and kept adding these isolates to their  database and we're using this to  determine whether a new positive result  was due to a new strain being introduced  into the facility or if these were or if  a new positive was due to one of the two  resonant strains that were in their  facility  in the past this really could not have  been done without identifying the  species of this listeria-like organism  uh that we were actually analyzing and  so ribo printer analysis uh actually  would have identified the species based  on the pattern assignment and so this  was really ideal for this facility to be  to continue tracking their strains  without determining the species and  implicating the new products using the  IR biotyper  so those were four different case  studies of how we have used the  technology in the past but we also do  see some future possibilities for how we  will use the technology going forward  one of the things that our team will  continue to do day in day out is to use  this on routine analysis of isolates  that come in and need to be subtyped in  order to determine if they are similar  or not I mean that's what we're  currently using it for right now we're  going to continue using it for that  however over time we would also like to  do things such as developing a database  of salmonella stereotypes and E coli o  groups to actually maybe potentially  provide a rapid serotyping library for  each of these isolates if one desires to  rapidly determine the exact zero type  grouping of the organism and do it  pretty quickly that would be nice  because you know we see a lot of things  the similar cell surface structures  between certain serotypes and we would  be able to group something in here  without having to use a lot of anti-sera  and doing a lot of extensive  agglutination testing to be able to  figure out what serotype it is  uh we also would be able to possibly do  side-by-side studies with banks of  isolates that have already been  subjected to whole genome sequencing in  order to determine the script  discriminatory power of the broker IR  biotyper as compared to the gold  standard microbial subtygiene method  whole genome sequencing we'd also like  to understand that and how those two  relate to each other  we also have thought about potentially  using this technology in a research  setting to see a different chemical  structures on the exterior of the  organism change over time that being the  same organism and seeing if they change  over time if they're subjected to  different conditions such as stressors  chemicals growth rate Etc  so that may actually alter some of that  surface chemistry and that would also be  kind of neat to be able to know if that  same sort of uh response is seen uh when  those conditions are applied to  different organisms or strains and  seeing if they have some sort of a shift  in their chemical struct